This page sets up the self-hosted editor for editors who are not developers. It is one Docker container: the image `ghcr.io/micacms/mica`. If you only edit on your own machine, you do not need this page. Use [Getting started](/docs/getting-started/).

> Mica is pre-1.0. Test upgrades and keep the version fixed in production.

## What you need

- A server with Docker and Docker Compose, and a reverse proxy that gives you HTTPS. Example: `https://cms.example.com`.
- A content repository on a Git host that you reach over HTTPS. github.com and gitlab.com are tested every week. Other hosts should work. It must contain the content model at `content/.mica/schema.json`. Your Astro build writes that file, and you commit it.
- An OpenID Connect provider. Mica keeps no passwords. If you have none, use Google: [Sign in with Google](/docs/sign-in-with-google/).

Before the first start, do these steps in this order. The self-hosted editor clones the branch of your repository. It needs the model in that branch:

1. Build the site once (`npx astro build`). This writes `content/.mica/schema.json`.
2. Commit the content root, `content/`, with `.mica/schema.json`. Push it to the branch that Mica uses.
3. Make the token (step 2 below).
4. Run the check: `docker compose run --rm mica check`. It tests read access, push rights with a dry run, and the model. A dry run does not test branch protection. Fix each error that it reports.
5. Start Mica (step 6 below), sign in, and open each collection once.

Self-hosted Mica keeps its own Git clone on its data volume. It talks to your Git host over HTTPS. SSH remotes are not supported yet. Git LFS under the content root is refused for now. To edit a checkout on your machine, use the local editor.

## 1. Get the two files

You do not clone the Mica repository. The image is published.

```bash
mkdir -p /srv/mica && cd /srv/mica
curl -o compose.yml https://raw.githubusercontent.com/micacms/mica/main/deploy/self-hosted/compose.yml
curl -o .env https://raw.githubusercontent.com/micacms/mica/main/deploy/self-hosted/.env.example
```

## 2. Connect the repository

Mica clones your repository and pushes to it over HTTPS. Make a token for that one repository.

For GitHub, make a fine-grained personal access token for that one repository with **Contents: read and write**. Give it nothing else.

For GitLab, make a project access token with the scopes **`read_repository`** and **`write_repository`**. Give it a role that may push to the branch. GitLab protects the default branch: if `MICA_REPOSITORY_BRANCH` is protected, give the token the **Maintainer** role. The **Developer** role cannot push to a protected branch. Give the token a short expiry.

```env
MICA_REPOSITORY_URL=https://github.com/acme/website.git
MICA_REPOSITORY_TOKEN=github_pat_...
MICA_REPOSITORY_BRANCH=main
MICA_REPOSITORY_ROOT=content
```

- `MICA_REPOSITORY_URL` is the HTTPS clone URL. Do not put credentials in it.
- `MICA_REPOSITORY_TOKEN` is the password for Git over HTTPS. Mica gives it only to this host and never writes it into the clone.
- `MICA_REPOSITORY_BRANCH` defaults to `main`.
- `MICA_REPOSITORY_ROOT` defaults to `content`. Mica writes only below the root, even if the token can write more.
- `MICA_REPOSITORY_USERNAME` is optional. The default depends on the host: `x-access-token` for GitHub, `oauth2` for GitLab, and `mica` for other hosts.
- `MICA_REPOSITORY_SYNC_SECONDS` is optional. It sets how often Mica fetches the branch. The default is 60.
- `MICA_DATA_DIR` is optional. It sets where Mica keeps its clone. The image uses the `/data` volume.

## 3. Connect the sign-in

Mica asks an OpenID Connect provider who a person is.

```env
MICA_OIDC_ISSUER=https://auth.example.com
MICA_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=mica
MICA_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
```

- `MICA_OIDC_ISSUER` must equal the `issuer` that the provider publishes at `<issuer>/.well-known/openid-configuration`.
- Omit `MICA_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET` for a public client that uses PKCE.
- Register one redirect URI with the provider: `<MICA_PUBLIC_URL>/api/auth/callback`.
- Mica asks for the scopes `openid profile email`. PKCE, state, and nonce are verified.

Step by step: [Sign in with Google](/docs/sign-in-with-google/) or [Other sign-in providers](/docs/other-providers/).

## 4. Say who may edit

Your provider knows many people. It does not know who may edit this website. Set at least one rule. Mica refuses to start without one.

```env
MICA_ADMIT_GROUP=mica-editors
MICA_ADMIT_DOMAIN=example.com
MICA_ADMIT_EMAILS=alice@gmail.com,bob@example.com
MICA_ADMIT_SUBJECTS=https://auth.example.com|a1b2c3
```

Each rule is a comma-separated list. One rule is enough.

- **Group:** the usual choice. Put a person in the group at your provider to add an editor. The claim name is `groups`. If your provider uses another name, set `MICA_OIDC_GROUPS_CLAIM`. A Keycloak path such as `/mica-editors` and the bare name both match.
- **Domain:** every address in a domain, but only addresses that the provider verified. Mica refuses a public domain such as `gmail.com` at start-up.
- **Emails:** these addresses, if the provider verified them. The comparison ignores case. Use this rule for Google accounts. One cost: if an organisation gives the address of a former editor to a new person, that person gets access. Gmail never reuses an address.
- **Subjects:** `issuer|subject` pairs. This is the strictest rule. A subject is never reassigned.

Everybody that a rule admits is an editor. There are no roles, no user table, and no invitations.

## 5. Set the address and the secret

```env
MICA_PUBLIC_URL=https://cms.example.com
TRUST_PROXY=true
SESSION_SECRET=
```

- `MICA_PUBLIC_URL` must be exactly what the browser shows. `https://` turns on Secure cookies. Mica refuses plain-HTTP discovery in production.
- Set `TRUST_PROXY=true` only behind a proxy that you control.
- `SESSION_SECRET` must have at least 32 characters. Make one:

```bash
node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
```

The container listens on `127.0.0.1:6422`. Put TLS in front of it. With Caddy, this is enough:

```text
cms.example.com {
    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:6422
}
```

## 6. Start and check

```bash
docker compose up -d
docker compose logs mica
curl -s https://cms.example.com/ready
```

You should see a start-up line that names the rule in force, and `/ready` should answer with `"status": "ok"`. Open `https://cms.example.com/admin/` and sign in.

If the container stops, read the log. Run `docker compose run --rm mica check` to see the cause. Mica stops at start-up when the repository URL or the token is missing, when it cannot make its first clone, when an old setting is in use, when no admission rule is set, or when the repository has no content model at `.mica/schema.json`. When the token cannot push, Mica starts and logs a warning, and `check` names the cause.

## Drafts, sync, and the data volume

A save is a draft. Drafts are files in the Git clone on the `/data` volume. Only editors see them. **Publish** commits all pending drafts as one commit and pushes it over HTTPS. The editor is the author of the commit. Mica is the committer. The push starts your site build, and the website build needs no CMS address and no token.

Mica syncs with the branch on its own. It fetches and fast-forwards. It never merges. If somebody changes an entry on the website while an editor has a draft of that entry, this is a conflict. The editor chooses **Keep my version** or **Use the website's version**.

The volume holds the clone and the unpublished drafts. If you lose it, unpublished work is gone. Nobody is signed out, and everything published is in the repository.

## Sessions and removal of access

A session is an encrypted cookie. Mica keeps no list of sessions.

```env
SESSION_TTL_HOURS=8
SESSION_IDLE_HOURS=4
```

- A session ends after 8 hours, or after 4 hours without use. While a session is in use, Mica issues the cookie again every 5 minutes.
- Sign-out clears the cookie in the browser only.
- Mica re-reads the rules on each request. The rules live in `.env`, so restart the container after a change. The change then counts at once.
- A change at your provider, for example removal from a group, counts from the next sign-in.
- To remove a person at once, take them out of the rules and restart. To end all sessions, change `SESSION_SECRET` and restart.

## Backups

The repository is the content backup, with the history of every publish. For unpublished work, back up the `/data` volume. First publish all drafts, or stop the container. Then copy the volume.

## Upgrades

The image tag in `compose.yml` is the version. Change it, then run:

```bash
docker compose pull && docker compose up -d
```

Read the changelog first, and pin a version.

When you change the content model, run the Astro build, commit `.mica/schema.json`, push, and restart:

```bash
docker compose restart mica
```

